<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calmecac</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The calmecac was a school for the sons of the Aztec nobility (pīpiltin) in the Late Postclassic period, where pupils entering at five to seven years of age lived and were trained in history, calendars, astronomy, religion, economy, law, ethics and warfare. Run by priests, it prepared students for the priesthood and for posts in government; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> records that commoners were at times admitted to train as priests, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> graduated from it. The calmecac of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> stood in the ceremonial centre near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> and was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>; archaeologists of INAH uncovered seven successive buildings there in the mid-2000s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calmecac.jpg" alt="Calmecac" width="330" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Gualpuyohualcal?; derivative work by El Comandante Hasta ∞ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calmecac_glyph_(Codex_Mendoza_61r).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noble boys were educated in priestly schools, the <b>calmecac</b>, which taught writing, the calendar, astronomy, songs and history.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Their sons were schooled at the <b>calmecac</b>, the temple school, in history, the calendar, law, oratory and war, and served as ambassadors, ministers, generals and priests.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
